I see your arguments... and as a sidenote, Mordor 1.1 looks like it
was made before wheels were invented.
Howabout DEMISE-Rise of the Ku'tan at http://decklinsdemise.com/screenshots.php?
Does that sort of thing grab anybody's attention? The 3d graphics
aren't exactly easy on my eyes (and personally I'd much prefer a 2d
approach with prettier gfx instead of poor 3d gfx), but the developer
definitely seems like he's progressed since the days of Mordor 1.1.
But yes, Gamespot gave it a terrible review and I believe them.

I am not exactly a roguelike vet, and correct me if I'm wrong, but the
appeal of roguelikes seems to me to be:

1. Randomly generated environments -> superior replay value
2. Large quantity of different kinds of items/monsters -> lots of
choices, new things to see and try
3. Intricate rules or mechanics -> you can knock the door down, you
can quaff the potion, throw the rock (or even your food rations), etc.
lots of options for any game situation. i remember in one game
(probably one of the old nethacks?) I would find shopkeepers in random
rooms in the dungeon, with their items strewn about the ground. if i
approached the wrong kind of armor or w/e (i think the symbol was
backwards), it would be a mimic and end up killing me. alternatively,
i could try and kill the shopkeeper with a wand or get my dog to steal
shop items. that sorta rich gameplay.

I've definitely played my share of Diablo (not so much Diablo 2). I
think what captured me (and perhaps many ppl) was finding items.
Magical items with properties based on randomly generated prefixes and
suffixes, a large collection of uniques, and (in diablo 2) crafted
items + set items. wow, you could even throw gems and runes into your
socketed items: so many items! there were lots of monsters too
(although most were just subtypes of a large handful of general
types), and nicer gfx than any other similar game i've ever seen.
still, I don't think Diablo offered the more intricate gameplay that
roguelikes were known for (true, there're a lotta diff spells, but
they just kill or shield, they don't blow down walls or destroy parts
of the dungeon).

another set of games that come to mind are: Baldur's Gate series,
Icewind Dale series, Planescape: Torment, (haven't personally tried
Neverwinter Nights series). These lacked randomly-generated
environments, but their detailed and thoughtful plots/stories were a
huge plus. Also, they had nice gfx/sound/music, a good helping of
monsters, and a variety of rules/mechanics ingame.

What is it about a roguelike that makes everyone a roguelike fan? What
are the most important aspects of making a roguelike? What aspects are
secondary?



